drills.explanation:
Purge first, then the question is only whether the id is still remembered:

```js
for (const [id, processedAt] of seen) {
  if (now - processedAt >= ttlMs) seen.delete(id);
}
if (seen.has(event.id)) return 'duplicate';
seen.set(event.id, now);
return 'process';
```

Deleting from a `Map` while iterating it is safe in JavaScript. The record is written only on the `'process'` branch, so the window always runs from the first processing. In production `seen` is a table with a unique event id and an expiry job, and the window is longer than the sender's whole retry schedule.

**What the tests catch.** The plausible wrong answer expires with `>` instead of `>=`, so a delivery at exactly `ttlMs` is still called a duplicate. Refreshing the time on every delivery lets a steady trickle of duplicates keep an id alive forever. Comparing the whole payload processes a redelivery whose attempt counter changed, and expiring only the incoming id lets `seen` grow without bound.
